# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `ThreadAbortException` are **not supported** in .NET 8 — calling `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. You must replace the cooperative-abort pattern with `CancellationToken`.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait for graceful completion with a timeout
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't finish in time — it's still running but
            // there is NO safe forced termination in .NET 8.
            // Log a warning; the token is already signaled.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** | There is no equivalent forced-kill mechanism in .NET 8. You *must* make all blocking calls cancellation-aware. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` removed** | No longer exists — remove entirely. |
| **Blocking `GetNextItem()` can't be cancelled** | If this wraps a `BlockingCollection`, use `TryTake(out item, -1, token)`. If it's I/O, switch to an async API that accepts `CancellationToken`. |
| **`volatile bool` is fragile** | `CancellationToken` is the idiomatic replacement — it integrates with async/await, `WaitHandle`, and framework APIs. |
| **No forced stop exists** | If code *truly* can't cooperate (e.g., calling unmanaged code that blocks forever), isolate it in a **separate process** and kill that process. In-process forced thread termination is gone by design. |

## If You Can't Make Everything Async

For a synchronous-only migration (e.g., `GetNextItem` can't change):

```csharp
private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        try
        {
            // Use an overload or wrapper that checks the token
            var item = GetNextItem(token);
            ProcessItem(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            break;
        }
        finally
        {
            FlushBuffers();
        }
    }
}
```

The key insight: every blocking call in the loop **must** accept and honor the `CancellationToken`, or your "stop" becomes a polite request that may hang indefinitely.
